so  determines that indeed there is liquid  in the audio  then downstream systems kick in like for  example natural language understanding  and other alexa responses  and actions  over the last one and a half years we  have shifted to  a different model architecture using  convolutional neural networks  here we use convolutional filters to  extract features from the acoustic  features  and then these are processed through  a few layers of convolutional networks  and then  through a fully connected neural network  and this is applied at the level of a  audio segment of almost at the word  level  and the advantage you get is that you  can  directly model a bunch of uh both  spectral and temporal context  to get more accurate detections as  is shown in one of these details where  we can see that compared to  the dna hmm system which is the blue  line  um we see a lower  false rejection rate as well as over  false excess with the cnn  and now we have you know propagated cnns  across our devices  across bakewards as well as vocals  now i'm going to walk you through a  sequence of papers that we're presenting  at interspeech  i do hope that you get a chance to talk  directly with the scientists  um to get more details and also to spur  interaction and collaboration the first  paper  is on what's called gain in variant  modeling  notice that we have 20 plus lxr devices  just manufactured within amazon  and we have many many more avs devices  each of this device has its own  microphone array  acoustic characteristics signal  processing in terms of beam forming echo  cancellation  so the audio front end introduces  variation in the audio gain which is  being input into the vacuum detector  and what we found was that this  variability in the audio gain  actually changes the model behavior for  instance  at the bottom right you can see some of  the dead curves where  if a different audio gain the dead curve  itself can shift  or even if it doesn't shift the  operating point on